// A CONTRACT THAT HASN'T RACED YET DOESN'T SPEND A SEASON. The simulator exposed this when run for four
// full races: 100% of signings broke at the very next rollover (22 signings / 22 breaks), the
// player lived in permanent drought, and it made NO difference whether you dominated or finished last, because nobody kept a seat.
//
// The cause: the window closes in DECEMBER and signs for the FOLLOWING year, but the contract was stamped with
// the CLOCK's year. In other words, you'd sign for a season that had already ended, and the rollover five days
// later would deduct its one and only season and kill it on January 5th.
//
// Tester feedback (Aug 27, [tester]) plus the Paddock WINDOW now respects phase and superlicense. Applying
// above your phase, or to a gated series without the license, is rejected at the front gate, and SIGNING
// a gated one dies at the single gate (AcceptRejectReason -> "superlicense") with the reason stated.


// 19/09 (Josh: «In February») — THE IVORY_CUP_12573 TEST IS THE PRESEASON PROLOGUE. It was on 14 June at Highgate, a
// 24 h, mid-season and the eve of the IVORY_CUP_12573 2024 Highgate (a clash for whoever runs LMGT3). The real Prologue
// is in late February in Qatar, before the first round. It is fixed: (1) 24 February at Fairworth Speedway (the substitute for
// Qatar already used by IVORY_CUP_12573 R1), 6 h of endurance like the NIMBUS_MASTERS_5890 test, before R1; (2) no clash with
// any IVORY_CUP_12573 2024 round, of either of its two classes; (3) the license chain in the SAME year: winning it opens
// ivory_trophy_7202; that day the market is sealed (Josh 18/09), and the window of that same year —which signs for the next one—
// opens later; with the license, the OFFERED Hypercar seat is signed in that year's decision week, without it
// not ("superlicense").


// Two halves of the same fix: SignWindowSeat stamps the DESTINATION season, and the rollover no longer deducts
// from someone who hasn't started yet.


// THE WINDOW SIGNS FOR NEXT YEAR, AND THE CLOCK DECIDES THAT -- NOT THE CALLER.
// Reported live by three players on 08/08: "I accept the offer in the signing window and it enrolls me in
// races for the year that's ALREADY passed; and afterwards there's no way to get a seat for the new year".
//
// The engine already knew how to do the right thing on both ends: AcceptOffer does NOT enroll when the contract
// is for a future season (its `year <= cc.year`), and the rollover neither deducts its season nor forgets to
// re-enroll it (its `signed_year >= cc.year + 1`). But both halves depended on WHOEVER SIGNS stamping
// the DESTINATION season, and the player's paths -- market, mail, negotiation -- were passing it the CLOCK's
// year, which during the window is the one that's ending. Exact result from the report: enrolled in the
// dead season, and a few days later at rollover the contract loses its one season and dies.
//
// The test that covered this (test_accept_offer) passed /*year=*/2026 BY HAND: it tested the engine already
// assuming the very translation that was broken. This one goes in through the door the player uses, the CLOCK.


// THE CHAMPIONSHIP TABLE WITH WHO ACTUALLY RACED. Reported on 09/08: "the drivers who raced
// with you (the ones in the XML) aren't the ones shown on the Championships tab". The table filled from two
// paths — rounds that were raced, with the REAL classification from the XML, and skipped ones, with SimulateRound — and each
// one built its own grid independently. Since points accumulate BY NAME, the table ended up being the
// UNION of two populations: drivers who were never on track mixed with those who were.
// The fix doesn't sync the two compositions (two synced things drift apart again): it removes the
// second source. If an XML-cast provider exists, the skipped round is simulated WITH THOSE drivers.


// RESCUING SAVES THAT ALREADY SIGNED WRONG (see the test above and market::SigningSeasonFor).
// Repairing other people's saves is irreversible, so the function is conservative and this test presses it from both
// sides: that it RECOVERS the contract from the failure, and above all that it does NOT TOUCH any legitimate ones that
// resemble it. The resemblance is real: a legitimate contract for the current season also carries
// signed_year == cc.year once December arrives, and confusing the two would hand it a free season.


// FOR SOMEONE WHO ALREADY LOST THEIR SEAT, ARE THINGS OKAY BY SEPTEMBER? The user's question on seeing the fix, and the
// answer had to be MEASURED, not just read: if the player whose contract the rollover killed can't sign again
// once the window reopens, the fix is worthless to them and something more needs to be done.
// What this test checks is that the way back EXISTS: with no contract, the clock reaches September, the
// window reopens, and you can sign. What nobody fixes is the season lost in between.


// THE CAREER DOESN'T DIE AT THE TOP. The "you already have a contract in that series" check was PER SERIES, not per
// series AND SEASON, and that turned reaching the top into a dead end: measured with the simulator,
// the player reached F1 in 2029 and in December 2030 was offered 24 seats that were exactly the ones in their three
// series. All 24 failed with "already", in January their contracts expired and they were left with nothing -- zero
// races in 2031 and game over. They couldn't move from Halcyon to Stonefield Motors because nothing checked whether their
// contract was running out.
//
// The two halves of the rule, which is what the user asked for: if your contract does NOT cover that season, signing
// is a SUCCESSION and costs nothing; if it DOES cover it, it's a BREACH and you decide it.


// THE WINDOW ALWAYS BRINGS SOMETHING WORTH WINNING. Silly season had no real contest: of 126 applications over six
// years only 4 ever got judged, and all four fell on the very first December. After that the world said
// yes to everything, and the verdict -- with its bar, its table and its cohort -- never got to race at all.
//
// The cause wasn't the on-ramp rule (that one's deliberate: at your level and below there's ALWAYS room), but
// WHAT MAKES IT INTO THE WINDOW. Candidates are ordered by CLOSENESS to your level, and closeness 0 is your OWN level,
// which is exactly what the on-ramp doesn't contest. Since more than MK_WINDOW_SEAT_CAP candidates already come from your own level,
// candidates, the cap ran out there and not a single seat above it got in.


// WHAT THE SEAT ASKS FOR, OUT LOUD. The candidacy model decided using the CANDFIT_W weights
