// THE LIVING-WORLD -> GRID BRIDGE. `ComposeSeasonGrid` pulled people from static files and never
// mentioned `SeatsForClass`, `seat_occupant` or `gen_drivers` even once, so the market and the track
// were talking about different people: the driver the world seats in a car did NOT race that race.
//
// It isn't theoretical, and it shows the moment you measure it: the teammate your own profile assigns you wasn't in
// the grid in 66 of the dominant profile's races, which is why the duel against them scored ZERO in three of the
// four profiles. With the bridge, those 66 drop to 14 and the duel becomes 98-0 / 97-3 / 7-11 / 5-13.


// THE TRACK LAGS BEHIND, AND YOU HAVE TO KNOW WHEN. AMS2 reads CustomAIDrivers ON STARTUP and never
// hot-reloads it, and the app lives INSIDE the game (the engine is what serves the API), so while
// you're playing AMS2 is ALWAYS open: a signing made in the window can't reach the track until you restart.
// Until now the rollover screen warned ALWAYS, unconditionally -- and warning always is the same as never warning.
//
// A FOOTPRINT of the seat assignment is compared against the one sealed when the XMLs were written, instead of
// storing a "restart needed" flag: a boolean has to be remembered both to set AND to clear, and it
// drifts out of sync the first time some path skips it. The footprint is checked against the CURRENT world.


// THE AI XML AND THE RACE'S SEATS ARE THE SAME NUMBER.
//
// There were TWO numbers that didn't talk to each other: the XML wrote ALL the class's liveries (66 in Coldstead Motors) and the
// race calculated its own seat count separately with a min(liveries, 26). In 37 of 110 classes they didn't match, and when
// there are extra names AMS2 CHOOSES which ones race -- a choice that isn't ours, so the championship kept
// changing rivals between races without warning.
//
// MEASURED IN-GAME (06/08, Oakfield Motors Zel380 with an XML trimmed to 5): the game picks FIRST from the liveries
// CustomAI names. With 4 seats and 5 names, four of ours showed up; with 23 seats and 5 names,
// it padded the rest with generics. In other words: as long as seats <= names, everyone on track is ours.


// ...AND THE OTHER CAP IS SET BY THE DRIVERS THEMSELVES. Class size says how many cars the championship HAS; it
// doesn't say whether there are people to drive them. Measured on 06/08: 13 series compose FEWER names than their class
// calls for -- GT4 calls for 45 and only lines up 26, Valemont Fal38 calls for 40 and lines up 27. And asking the race for 45 seats against
// a 26-name XML returns EXACTLY the generic auto-fill that the single-number rule was meant to remove.
//
// The authority is the composition itself, not a parallel formula ("pinned + cohort + field") that would
// drift out of sync the first time someone touches the seat assignment.


// THE SEATS THE RACE ASKS FOR AGAINST THE NAMES IN THE XML. The definitive check, and the
// only one that can't be fooled: it calls the PRODUCTION WRITER, counts what it actually wrote per class,
// and compares it against what the launch is going to ask for.
//
// It's needed because the XML is PER CLASS and some classes race in TWO series (GT4 = harbor_championship_5234 + onyx_cup_5193; Onyx_Cup_4824 =
// nimbus_masters_5890 + lantern_trophy_5644; Pemberton_Zel121 = ivory_cup_12573 + nimbus_masters_5890), so a single file has to serve both. And because the writer
// applies multiclass EXCLUSION -- a driver already placed in a faster class of the same weekend isn't repeated --
// so it leaves FEWER names than the series would compose on its own. Measured on disk on 06/08: GT4.xml with 18
// names while harbor_championship_5234 called for 26. Eight generics, silently.
// ISO-3166-1 alpha-3, the table AMS2 knows. It lived inside the flags test of the and now
