// ===================================================================================================
// 16/09 — THE FLAG'S SOURCE: valid country, one card per person, and not a single HTML entity
// ===================================================================================================
//
// Column 9 of `docs/career/f3_authored_profiles.psv` exists because without it the AUTHORED RESERVE is
// seated and cannot race: `career_registry` registers everyone who has a card here and does not run in
// any grid today, the market seats them, and the composition did not even know their name — 71 rows
// lost at five seasons and 115 at twenty (`--sinnombre`). With the name resolved but no flag the
// remedy would be worse: AMS2 SILENTLY discards a `<country>` that its table does not know and the driver comes out with
// the wrong flag, which is exactly the .
//
// Three things have to be guaranteed about that data, and nothing covered any of them:
//
//  (1) **Every written country is a valid ISO-3166-1 alpha-3.** It is the same requirement the flags test of the
//      places on the COMPOSED grid, brought to the SOURCE. So that a typo falls in the PSV and not three
//      layers further down. Empty IS allowed: it means «resolves by another route, or their country is yet to be decided».
//  (2) **One person, one card.** The corpus is indexed by FOLDED name (`FoldDriverName`), so two
//      rows whose name folds the same are the SAME key and the last one read silently wins.
//  (3) **No name carries an HTML entity.** And it is not hypothetical: eight rows arrived with `&;` (an
//      unescaped non-breaking space) inside the name —«Kelvin van&;der&;Linde»—, and since the folding
//      keeps the DIGITS, `kelvinvan160der160linde` and `kelvinvanderlinde` were two different people.
//      Three of those eight already had their clean row in this same file: they were invisible duplicates. Checks
//      (2) and (3) are the ones that would have warned.
// ===================================================================================================
// 16/09 — THE SPELLING LOCK: the same person cannot have two identities
// ===================================================================================================
//
// This test exists because the failure was found BY HAND and should not have needed to be.
//
// An id is minted from the RAW name (`PackDriverId`, `FieldOccupantId` and `PinnedOccupantId` fall back to a hash
// FNV of the name when the person is not in the pool). The registry's name index, on the other hand, is
// queried FOLDED (`FoldDriverName`: no accents, no signs, lowercase). Those two rules together have
// an exact crack: **two files that write the same person with a different spelling produce two
// identities**, and neither of them knows it.
//
// It has happened twice, with the two ways a name can be misspelled:
//   · **by HTML entity**: `f3_authored_profiles.psv` carried «Kelvin van&;der&;Linde» — the folding
//     keeps the DIGITS, so `kelvinvan160der160linde` did not match `kelvinvanderlinde`;
//   · **by accent**: the corpus wrote «Caca Bueno» and `pack_drivers.psv` «Cacá Bueno».
// In both cases the ghost occupied a seat, nobody knew it, and its car did not go out on track.
//
// The rule, and it is simple: if two identities fold to the SAME name, they are either the same person written two
// ways —and then one is surplus— or they are two real namesakes, and then they are written IDENTICALLY letter for
// letter. What cannot exist is the middle ground.
//
// Legitimate namesakes do NOT fail here: «Joao Barbosa» (BRA) and «Joao Barbosa» (PRT) are two
// different people with the same name and their two rows are written identically; the tie-break by country is already done by
// `IdForName`. This test only forbids the divergent spelling.
// 23/09 — THE NON-BREAKING SPACE. «Nelson Piquet Jr.» came with U+00A0 between «Piquet» and «Jr.» in 83 rows of
// livery_resolved.psv and in the pack label «Ashmere Racing»: on screen it does not show, but
// the other table (pack_team_class.psv) writes that label with a normal space and the label did not find its
// curated team. Every person or team name is cleaned when LOADED (CleanNameSpaces, career_text.h).








// NOBODY ELSE DRIVES YOUR LIVERY. The crossover with seats only RENAMES drivers by livery, it never
// removes one: the livery you occupy kept showing up in the AI grid under the driver its static plumbing
// had assigned. Two cars with the same paint scheme -- and with 23 names for 22 rivals, AMS2 picks on its own
// which one to leave out, which might not be that one. The intent had been written into career_seats for a while
// ("one car, one driver") and wasn't implemented; the user found it by reasoning, not a test.
//
// It gets REPLACED with another free livery, not trimmed: the class almost always has more liveries than seats.


// A MULTICLASS WEEKEND ASKS FOR THE MEETING'S CARS, NOT YOUR CLASS'S. This launched the race with the
// PLAYER'S class grid size: on a IVORY_CUP_12573 weekend, 18 seats (Pemberton_Zel121's) split across
// three classes = 6 cars per class. The real number is the CHAMPIONSHIP's -- the IVORY_CUP_12573 lines up 36 -- and the
// equal split is already handled by the game's own builder fix (an internal engine routine divides total/n).
//
// The shortest class's cap governs all of them, and it isn't cosmetic: Garage 56 has 2 liveries and as the
// IVORY_CUP_12573's fourth class it left the whole weekend at 8 cars (2 per class). That's why it was dropped from the group.


// HOW MANY CARS GO OUT IN A SPECIAL EVENT IS DECIDED BY ITS AUTHOR, NOT BY THE CLASS YOU PICK. Reported on
// 14/09: in the Irondale Seca 500 (beacon_series_4332 + cinder_series_4291 + pinnacle_masters_4414) 20 cars went out picking GT1 90s, 16 with GT1
// 2005 and 12 with QUENBY QUO872. The engine asked for `GridSizeForSeries(chosen class)` and in multiclass that number is the
// meeting TOTAL, which the game's builder splits in equal parts: the player changed the whole event
// with the car dropdown. The calendar never had the failure (it asks for MeetingGridSize).
//
// What this test fixes is the complete ladder: the author's `overrides.grid` rules, the player's car cap
// (, which is a limit of THEIR machine) rules over the author, and without `grid` multiclass goes back to asking for
// the meeting -- which is the same number whichever class is chosen.


// NO RACE OF THE CALENDAR ASKS FOR MORE CARS THAN FIT ON ITS TRACK.
//
// Measured on 16/09 with the real slot counts of the 230 installed tracks: **52 of the 1.238 races of the
// calendar asked for more cars than the circuit has**. Stock USA lined up 40 at Coldton Bay (32 slots),
// the 60s touring cars thirty at Northhurst Park 1971 (26) and the Nimbus Trophy 4168 forty at Westvale Speedway (26).
// Nobody had ever noticed because the engine had NOT A SINGLE track capacity datum: the number was asked
// of the CLASS and the circuit had no say.
//
// Two things were fixed at once and both are guarded here:
//   the three rallycross series (RX, Greywick Motors, Pinnacle Championship) asked for 16-20 cars on EIGHT-slot tracks in their
//     ENTIRE calendar: there the series number was wrong, and it was lowered to 8 (class_grid_size.psv).
//   the rest were one-off dates of long calendars, and those get the ROUND capped (RoundGridSize), without
//     touching either the series size or the curated calendar -- removing Northhurst Park 1971 from the Keystone_Masters_11138 calendar
//     over four cars on one date out of fifteen would be worse than the problem.
//
// This test is the lock: it walks the ENTIRE calendar and fails if a SINGLE round exceeds its track. It also
// LISTS the ones the cap trims, which are exactly those that would have slipped through without it.


// THE «HOME» BRAZIL ENDURANCE IS A MEETING OF 40 (Josh's decision of 16/09, which changes that of 29/08).
//
// The four (onyx_championship_8842, juniper_masters_8924, ember_series_8965, onyx_championship_9006) share the exact FIFTEEN rounds -- same track, same day, and
// no other series on those dates -- so they were already a meeting in everything but the split. Until 16/09
// they ran as four single-class races of 20/15/20/20 by decision of 29/08 («both experiences»); see
// the long note in test_multiclass_groups_data, which keeps the old decision and why it changes.
//
// What this test measures is what Josh approved with the figures in front of him: 40 cars on ten dates and the track
// cap trimming on the other five. And the two things that CANNOT break when grouping: that each series
// still has ITS table with ITS points, and that the split is not capped by the liveries of the shortest class.


// A SKIPPED MULTICLASS MEETING DOES NOT PUT OTHER CLASSES IN YOUR TABLE. Measured on 15/09 with the double and a save from
// 2043: the IVORY_CUP_12573 round at Millvale simulated 54 cars, all three classes labeled as LANTERN_MASTERS_7489, and the table of
// lantern_trophy_7694 ended up with 55 drivers, 14 of MERROWDOWN_MYR373 and 18 of Pemberton_Zel121. Two pieces: the XML provider joined the classes of
// the meeting (it now gives only the series') and SimulateRound did not trim to the class's share (now it does). The whole
// meeting, for whoever puts it on track without the game, is assembled by SimulateMeeting class by class.


// AND THE TABLES THAT ALREADY GOT DIRTY ARE REPAIRED ONCE. A 0.6.x save carries meeting rounds that you
// skipped with all three classes scoring (test save of 2043: lantern_trophy_7694 with 52 drivers and a MERROWDOWN_MYR373 leader). Those
// rounds are re-simulated and only those; your row, the rounds you ran and the ones that are clean are not touched, and a
// second load does nothing. Synthetic fixture: an lantern_trophy_7694 season saved and loaded like a real one.


// RATING HAS A FLOOR, AND WHAT YOU EARNED DOESN'T GET ERASED. Two flaws in the rating model that only
// showed up by running the multi-year simulator over several years, and that were masking each other.
//
// (1) FLOOR. `prog.rating += delta` had no lower bound: measured, -19.1 after two years averaging P22
//     finishes. A negative ELO isn't "very bad", it's a number outside the domain the model declares for
//     itself (RATING_BAND_MIN[0] == 0.0: the lowest band STARTS at zero). And it isn't cosmetic -- RatingQuality
//     feeds the player's pace in the career model, so below the floor it keeps subtracting
//     without quality being able to drop any further: invisible debt that has to be paid back before a comeback shows.
//
// (2) THE HEAL WAS ERASING EARNED RATING. On load, a rating < 900 got reseeded to 1250 as "corrupt". That
//     made sense for the case that motivated it (rating ~32 after ONE race, from starting unseeded), but the
//     VALUE-based threshold can't tell corruption from "you're doing badly": a player finishing P18 of 27 legitimately
//     drops to 389 over 75 races, and every load reset it back to 1250. Progression didn't exist, and the bug was
//     invisible because the number always looked healthy.


// OFFERS YOU DIDN'T TAKE GET TAKEN BY SOMEONE ELSE. User's rule: "once closed, they get filled".
//
// A seat offered and not signed stayed OFFERED until the September window cleared the
// board, and NOBODY occupied the seat. Three consequences, none cosmetic: the world was left with an
// empty seat, the screen kept showing a dead offer as if it were a way out, and InDrought
// counted it as "you have something on the table", so the drought was never declared.


