// the name. The lock: after the winter, the winner occupies a seat of THAT team, occupies ONE single seat in the whole
// world, and the incumbent they displace has not vanished (is still seated elsewhere, waits as a free agent
// or appears as retired).


// 23/09 — THE SEAT KEYS THAT AN EARLIER VERSION BREAKS. Seen on a test save: 73 lines
// `seatocc juniper_series|178530063571rd_4012492869` and `seatstart juniper_series|2147483647`. It is 0.6.x reading a
// 0.7.0 save: it splits at the FIRST '|' and glues the rest without separators, and on saving leaves the bare series
// as the key. The lock: the good key is rebuilt (the slot whose numeric prefix fits), what points
// to no seat is deleted, and a second pass touches nothing.


// THE OFFER EXPIRES -- reported by a player while testing: "contracts open in March, when there
// shouldn't be contracts of any kind".
//
// And it wasn't cosmetic. Sealed seats only get cleared when the NEXT window OPENS (September), so
// from the December decision week they'd sit in the save through the entire winter and spring. Worse:
// SignWindowSeat didn't check the date, only that the state was OFFERED. You could sign in March a seat
// offered in December -- signing for a season that had already been running for three months.
//
// The limit that gets set: you can sign until the season you're signing for KICKS OFF. In winter the
// market still makes sense; once the cars are already on track, it doesn't.


// A VERDICT IS FINAL -- exposed by the simulator on its first long run.
// The decision week lasts SEVEN days and the market pulse runs every time the clock advances, so the
// same seat got judged once per pulse. You could see it plainly in the timeline:
//
//     2025/349  offered   beacon_cup  Aurora Grand Prix
//     2025/349  signed    beacon_cup  Aurora Grand Prix
//     2025/356  rejected  beacon_cup  Aurora Grand Prix   Finn Carrow
//
// You sign the seat and a week later it's taken from you, named after whoever gets it. In the game
// it's less noticeable because you have to advance the clock by hand within that week, but the bug is the same.


// THE SIMULATOR, STITCHED TOGETHER. The simulator was split into two halves that never touched:
//   · SimulateYears runs races but jumps year to year WITHOUT touching the clock, so the signing window
//     never opens and the entire silly season is invisible there.
//   · advance-week moves the clock through the app's own path (and with it the whole world happens), but NOBODY RACES:
//     the player accumulates absences, at three the contract auto-breaks, and within a few weeks the career
//     is dead and everything you measure afterward is garbage.
//
// SimulateWorld stitches them together. What this test fixes is the rule everything else hangs on:
//
//   RACE BEFORE ADVANCING. And it doesn't fail loudly if you invert it: it fails by leaving a simulation that
//   LOOKS like it's working while the career quietly dies. That's why the assertion isn't "doesn't crash", it's that after
//   40 weeks under contract the absence counter is still ZERO.


// THE DROUGHT: the window passed and you were left with nothing.
// It isn't game over, which is why it has its own name instead of being the absence of something: it's THE FLOOR. You rent,
// race a season at your own expense, and in September you get looked at again. Until now this state didn't exist, so
// the tab stayed empty, which reads as "the game has frozen" rather than "you've been left out".


// , the back-door exit -- LEAVING COSTS YOU, AND SO DOES MISSING RACES.
// Breaking a contract only penalized REP and nothing else, so signing more than you needed and walking away cost nothing in credits:
// exactly the opposite of what's wanted: calendar management should be the player's call. And absences
// dinged the rating and broke the contract on the third one, but in between they didn't cost you the seat: you could
// hit the target while missing races and still get renewed just the same.


// THE TEAMMATE EXISTS. Until today it didn't exist in any form: zero
// appearances in the engine. Without one, the "beat your teammate" clause can't even be stated, because
// there's no one to point to.
//
// It's the other seat carrying YOUR SAME label in the same series, derived from SeatsForClass and never persisted:
// if the world moves it mid-season, the answer changes on its own.


// 05/08, the verdict -- YOU HIT THE TARGET AND GET RENEWED, YOU MISS IT AND THEY LOOK FOR SOMEONE ELSE.
// Your seat already entered the window, but the decision week judged you like a stranger: by the shortlist
// of candidates, ignoring the target the team set for you when you signed. Now your seat isn't contested -- the
// team already knows how you've performed --, and what decides is obj_target_pos measured with the SAME formula as the
// rollover (PlayerSeasonRatios -> ContractSeasonRatio, pivot 0.65 = "you just barely hit it").
//
// The ratio comes in as a parameter and isn't computed in here on purpose: the championship table lives in the
// calendar and the window lives in seats. Passing it in keeps ONE formula instead of two that look alike.


// 05/08 -- YOUR CONTRACT ENTERS THROUGH THE SAME WINDOW. The whole world rotated except you: the
// "is this seat in play" check asked about the contract DERIVED from the driver the data seats in
// that seat, and in yours that driver is a ghost (the seat model doesn't know you're there, as PlayerSeatKeys'
// own comment says). So your seat went to the window whenever some other driver's imaginary contract
// expired, or it never went at all.
//
// What expires on your seat is YOUR contract. With seasons still ahead the seat isn't in play; on the
// last one, it is, and it's contested like the others. That's what this means by "entering through the same window".


// 05/08 -- THE SEAT YOU SIGN IS YOURS. `ct.seat_idx` was hardcoded to 0 in
// AcceptOffer: whoever you signed with, the engine recorded that you occupied seat 0 of the series.
//
// It isn't cosmetic, and you can see it by reading the two places that consume it:
//   PlayerSeatKeys shields SeatKey(series, seat_idx) against the living world's cascade (attrition, relegation,
//     rookies). With the 0 hardcoded it shielded ANOTHER team's seat, and yours was left at the mercy of the
//     cascade -- exactly the bug an earlier fix claimed to have closed.
//   The rollover writes kSeatOpen at THAT index when you're not renewed (career_calendar.cpp:1432), meaning
//     leaving opened up the seat for a team that wasn't yours, while yours stayed occupied.
//
// The invariant fixed here is the one that needs to hold for "your seat enters the window" to
// mean anything: SeatsForClass(series)[ct.seat_idx].team_label == ct.team_id.


// 05/08 -- YOU SIGN WITH ONE TEAM AND DRIVE ANOTHER'S CAR. Reported while testing: the player signed with
// team "3422" of the Garrowby Nox98 and in the pre-race screen got the wrong thumbnail. The signing itself doesn't fail: what fails is the
// REVERSE team->livery crossover (TeamSeatOptionsFor, which ResolveTeamSeat hangs off of). It consulted
// livery_team_equiv and livery_vehicle_map, but NEVER the resolved layer (livery_resolved.psv) -- exactly the one that now
// GOVERNS naming (layer 0 of TeamLabelFor). A team named by the resolved layer didn't exist for the
// reverse crossover: TeamSeatOptionsFor returned empty and ResolveTeamSeat fell back to its "fixed-random"
// (seed % vehs.size()), which gives a stable car+skin... from a different team.
//
// The shape of the bug: the two halves of the SAME question were reading different tables. The team's NAME came
// from livery_resolved; its CAR, from livery_team_equiv. As long as the two agree nobody notices; the moment the
// resolved layer covers a class the equiv table doesn't have (Garrowby Nox98: 0 rows in equiv, 26 in the vehicle map), it
// splits in two. The fix is to have the reverse crossover read the SAME layer 0 as the forward one, in the same order.
//
// And why invariant (b) of test_team_seat_options stayed GREEN with this broken: it builds its list of
// classes by reading livery_team_equiv.psv, and Garrowby Nox98 isn't in there. It measured the fixed-random behavior of the classes that DID
// have equiv data and was blind to the 2948 rows in the resolved layer. The invariant here walks the
// RESOLVED layer, the one that governs -- otherwise it stays green while measuring the side that isn't broken.


// (team curation): TeamSeatOptionsFor resolves a real team's OWN car+skin — the equiv table
// inverted BY LABEL, crossed with livery_vehicle_map.psv (exact livery -> vehHash+NN from the official
// dump). Loads THIS repo's docs via relative paths (the runner cd's to the repo root). Invariants:
//   (a) the vehicle map loads (~2800 rows);
//   (b) for EVERY curated class, EVERY label TeamLabelsForClass surfaces resolves to >=1 curated option
//       (an offer from a curated class NEVER falls back to the fixed-random seat);
//   (c) known team -> its real car+NN (MERROWDOWN_MYR373 Rothbury Racing fields 2 C8.R cars, NN 50/51);
//   (d) a raw-livery label (pinned class without equiv rows) maps 1:1 through path (2);
//   (e) unknown team / empty label -> empty (the caller keeps its legacy fallback);
//   (f) deterministic (same inputs -> same options).


// 05/08: HAND-CURATED DATA BEATS THE FEEDER MAP. Reported while testing: Garrowby Nox98 showed teams
// the user hadn't created, Copa TSI showed "Merrowdown Cor168" (the CAR) as the team, and Formula Vee showed
// DRIVER NAMES. A single cause for all three: the feeder map covers 626 liveries from the same generation
// classes that now have a row in livery_resolved.psv, and it sat AHEAD in the cascade.
//
// The code's own comment already warned that the two datasets should be disjoint and that, if they
// overlapped, the feeder would win "on purpose". They overlapped the moment the resolved layer covered those classes.


// Mirrors SeatsForClass's naming cascade: the CURATED data (livery_resolved.psv) governs and the feeder map
// sits below it. The grid<->seats alignment invariants consult this to measure the
// ALIGNMENT, not the precedence -- if another layer gets added tomorrow, only this helper needs touching, not three tests.
static std::string EsperadoEquipoDeLibrea(const std::string& cls, const std::string& liv) {
    std::string r = career::ResolvedTeamFor(cls, liv);
    return !r.empty() ? r : career::FeederTeamFor(cls, liv);
}

